  "metadata": {
    "title": "Software stocks whipsaw amid AI fears, as Anthropic IPO looms",
    "byline": null,
    "published_at": null,
    "site_name": "finance.yahoo.com",
    "language": null
  },
  "content": {
    "text": "The software sector is currently caught in a tug-of-war between profound optimism and existential dread over the future of artificial intelligence. After a brief period of euphoria, reality—and algorithmic trading—bit back hard, sending major software stocks tumbling in a sudden reversal.\nThe iShares Expanded Tech-Software Sector ETF fell 3%, wiping out half of the 6% gains it had recorded just a day prior. The bleeding was widespread across the industry’s biggest names: Atlassian plunged 8%, HubSpot and Okta both dropped 7%, and ServiceNow and Intuit each shed 6%. Rubrik and Workday followed close behind with a 5% drop, while heavyweights like Microsoft, Salesforce, Oracle, Datadog, Cloudflare, and Palantir fell between 3% and 4%.\nTo understand the intense volatility in the software sector, one must look at the rapidly evolving narrative around \"agentic AI.\" As artificial intelligence moves from simple chatbots to autonomous agents capable of executing multi-step workflows, a chilling question has gripped Wall Street: If AI can simply do the work for us, will businesses still need to buy dozens of traditional software subscriptions?\nFor months, the fear has been that AI agents might bypass traditional Software-as-a-Service (SaaS) platforms entirely, causing mass disruption to the business models of companies like Salesforce, Workday, and Atlassian.\nHowever, software stocks experienced a massive surge on Monday following comments from Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang, who strongly pushed back against this doom-and-gloom narrative. Speaking directly to the fears of industry obsolescence, Huang painted a picture of an AI-driven future where software companies are the ultimate beneficiaries.\n\"This is actually an incredible time to be a software company,\" Huang said during his presentation. \"A lot of people have said, Jensen, agentic AI is coming, therefore all of the software companies are going out of business. I said: it’s exactly the opposite. Because there are going to be so many agents, the world is no longer limited by the number of people. Therefore, those agents are going to use more tools than ever.\"\nHuang’s thesis—that AI agents will act as a massive multiplier for software tool usage—temporarily quelled investor anxiety, sparking Monday’s 6% sector-wide rally.\nYet, that confidence evaporated almost overnight. Analysts struggled to find a fundamental catalyst for the sudden, widespread sell-off that hammered the sector the very next day. Instead of shifting fundamentals, market mechanics may be to blame for the whiplash.",
    "text_blocks": [
      "The software sector is currently caught in a tug-of-war between profound optimism and existential dread over the future of artificial intelligence. After a brief period of euphoria, reality—and algorithmic trading—bit back hard, sending major software stocks tumbling in a sudden reversal.",
      "The iShares Expanded Tech-Software Sector ETF fell 3%, wiping out half of the 6% gains it had recorded just a day prior. The bleeding was widespread across the industry’s biggest names: Atlassian plunged 8%, HubSpot and Okta both dropped 7%, and ServiceNow and Intuit each shed 6%. Rubrik and Workday followed close behind with a 5% drop, while heavyweights like Microsoft, Salesforce, Oracle, Datadog, Cloudflare, and Palantir fell between 3% and 4%.",
      "To understand the intense volatility in the software sector, one must look at the rapidly evolving narrative around \"agentic AI.\" As artificial intelligence moves from simple chatbots to autonomous agents capable of executing multi-step workflows, a chilling question has gripped Wall Street: If AI can simply do the work for us, will businesses still need to buy dozens of traditional software subscriptions?",
      "For months, the fear has been that AI agents might bypass traditional Software-as-a-Service (SaaS) platforms entirely, causing mass disruption to the business models of companies like Salesforce, Workday, and Atlassian.",
      "However, software stocks experienced a massive surge on Monday following comments from Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang, who strongly pushed back against this doom-and-gloom narrative. Speaking directly to the fears of industry obsolescence, Huang painted a picture of an AI-driven future where software companies are the ultimate beneficiaries.",
      "\"This is actually an incredible time to be a software company,\" Huang said during his presentation. \"A lot of people have said, Jensen, agentic AI is coming, therefore all of the software companies are going out of business. I said: it’s exactly the opposite. Because there are going to be so many agents, the world is no longer limited by the number of people. Therefore, those agents are going to use more tools than ever.\"",
      "Huang’s thesis—that AI agents will act as a massive multiplier for software tool usage—temporarily quelled investor anxiety, sparking Monday’s 6% sector-wide rally.",
      "Yet, that confidence evaporated almost overnight. Analysts struggled to find a fundamental catalyst for the sudden, widespread sell-off that hammered the sector the very next day. Instead of shifting fundamentals, market mechanics may be to blame for the whiplash."
